  "answerSpec": {
   "value": 12
  },
  "explanation": "$\\displaystyle\\sum_{r=1}^{n} (2r + 3) = 2 \\cdot \\frac{n(n + 1)}{2} + 3n = n^2 + 4n$. Then $n^2 + 4n - 192 = 0$, so $(n + 16)(n - 12) = 0$ and $n = 12$ (as $n > 0$).",
  "workedSolution": "1. $\\displaystyle\\sum_{r=1}^{n} (2r + 3) = 2 \\cdot \\frac{n(n + 1)}{2} + 3n = n^2 + 4n$. Then $n^2 + 4n - 192 = 0$, so $(n + 16)(n - 12) = 0$ and $n = 12$ (as $n > 0$).",
