 {
  "key": "series.sigma-notation.q16",
  "type": "STRUCTURED",
  "concepts": [
   "amath.series.sigma-notation"
  ],
  "difficulty": 4,
  "marks": 6,
  "estimatedSeconds": 540,
  "stem": "This question is about $\\displaystyle\\sum_{r=1}^{n} (3r - 2)$.",
  "parts": [
   {
    "label": "(a)",
    "type": "EXTENDED_RESPONSE",
    "marks": 2,
    "markingMode": "MANUAL",
    "stem": "Show that $\\displaystyle\\sum_{r=1}^{n} (3r - 2) = \\frac{n(3n - 1)}{2}$.",
    "answerSpec": {
     "markScheme": {
      "points": [
       {
        "id": "p1",
        "description": "M1 uses $3\\sum r - \\sum 2 = 3 \\cdot \\frac{n(n + 1)}{2} - 2n$.",
        "marks": 1
       },
       {
        "id": "p2",
        "description": "A1 simplifies to $\\frac{3n^2 + 3n - 4n}{2} = \\frac{n(3n - 1)}{2}$.",
        "marks": 1
       }
      ]
     },
     "modelAnswer": "$$\n\\sum_{r=1}^{n} (3r - 2) = 3\\sum_{r=1}^{n} r - \\sum_{r=1}^{n} 2 = \\frac{3n(n + 1)}{2} - 2n\n$$\n\n$$\n= \\frac{3n^2 + 3n - 4n}{2} = \\frac{3n^2 - n}{2} = \\frac{n(3n - 1)}{2}\n$$"
    },
    "explanation": "Split the sum, then use Σr = n(n + 1)/2 and Σc = nc."
   },
   {
    "label": "(b)",
    "type": "NUMERICAL",
    "marks": 1,
    "stem": "Hence evaluate $\\displaystyle\\sum_{r=1}^{30} (3r - 2)$.",
    "answerSpec": {
     "value": 1335
    },
    "explanation": "$\\frac{30(89)}{2} = 1335$.",
    "workedSolution": "1. $\\frac{30(89)}{2} = 1335$."
   },
